# PasswordField — a real password box, in the same `Field` scaffold as every
# other field in this library.
#
#     state pw: text = "" @client
#     state pwShown: bool = false @client
#     use PasswordField("Password", "", true, "At least 12 characters.", failed(signIn), pw, pwShown)
#
# `password` is a control now — see the `password` row of `ast.Controls` in
# fct/internal/ast/ast.go. It lowers to the same `input` IR node `input` itself
# does, on the same `text` cell contract, plus one attribute pair a browser
# reads: `type="password"`, which masks the box and gives it the browser's own
# reveal affordance, and `autocomplete="current-password"`, which is what tells
# a password manager to fill the account's EXISTING secret. That is what this
# component renders now. Nothing here is painted with CSS any more — the old
# `-webkit-text-security` mask, and the doc comment explaining what it could
# not buy, are both gone because neither is true any more.
#
# `current-password` IS THE RIGHT TOKEN HERE AND ONLY HERE. A sign-up box or a
# change-password box wants the OTHER token, `new-password` — it tells a
# manager to offer to GENERATE a secret instead of filling the old one, and
# `current-password` on that box is a real defect (see `ast.Controls`'s own
# comment on the two keywords). A control keyword is not an expression, so
# nothing at a call site can choose between the two at runtime; this component
# only ever writes `current-password`, and `NewPasswordField` below is the
# other one. Use this one for sign-in. Use that one for sign-up and
# change-password. Do not reach for this one because it is already imported.
#
# ── `shown: cell bool` IS STILL A PARAMETER, AND IT NO LONGER DOES ANYTHING ──
#
# The old version of this component masked the box with CSS and read a
# checkbox's state with `:has()` to unmask it — a trick CSS can do because CSS
# can style anything. A real `type="password"` input is not stylable that way:
# the browser owns the masking, not the page, so there is no rule that reveals
# it from the outside. Nor can the control be switched to a plain `input` at
# runtime — which keyword a control is lowers at compile time, from the word
# the author wrote, not from an expression over a cell; there is no attribute
# escape hatch either, so neither renderer has anywhere to read "revealed" from.
#
# The only way left to build a reveal is two separate elements bound to the
# same cell, one `password` and one `input`, swapped by an `if` on `shown` —
# and that is the exact failure this workspace has already hit for a different
# reason: `apps/journal/pages/signin.fct`'s own comment notes that two inputs
# on one cell "visibly disagree while typing, because the client syncs only
# the input that is not focused." Toggling that on every keystroke of a
# password box is worse than shipping no reveal at all, so the reveal is
# removed rather than left broken.
#
# `shown` stays in the parameter list anyway, because two apps in this
# workspace already call `PasswordField` with a seventh argument, and changing
# the arity out from under a caller is a worse break than one dead parameter.
# Nothing in this component reads it any more. It is an ordinary `@client` bool
# the caller owns; a host that wants a reveal badly enough to accept the
# focus-loss trade-off above is free to build one beside the field with it.
import "../forms/field.fct"
app FacetAuth:
    component PasswordField(label: text, placeholder: text, required: bool, hint: text, error: text, v: cell text, shown: cell bool):
        use Field(label, required, hint, error):
            password bind v placeholder "{placeholder}"
    # NewPasswordField — the same box, `new-password` in place of
    # `current-password`. For a sign-up form's password box, and for any
    # change-password / set-password surface: the field where a password
    # manager should be offered the chance to GENERATE a secret rather than be
    # asked to fill the account's existing one. It takes no `shown` parameter —
    # it is new, nothing depends on its arity yet, and the reveal it would carry
    # is exactly as inexpressible as `PasswordField`'s (see above). See
    # `auth/signupform.fct` for the call site this exists for.
    component NewPasswordField(label: text, placeholder: text, required: bool, hint: text, error: text, v: cell text):
        use Field(label, required, hint, error):
            newpassword bind v placeholder "{placeholder}"
